{"causes":["Published between 1999 and 2000, marking a new era in fantasy literature.","George R.R. Martin's unique blend of politics, history, and mythology influenced the direction of modern epic fantasy.","The rise of urban fantasy and paranormal romance can be partially attributed to A Storm of Swords' groundbreaking storytelling and character development."],"effects":["Influenced a generation of fantasy authors, including Patrick Rothfuss and Joe Abercrombie.","Shaped the television adaptation 'Game of Thrones', which premiered in 2011 and became a global phenomenon.","Contributed to the popularity of geek culture and fandom, with fans worldwide eagerly anticipating each new installment in the series."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"george-r-r-martin","sourceName":"George R.R. Martin","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-storm-of-swords","targetName":"A Storm of Swords","context":"Martin's masterpiece is widely regarded as one of the greatest novels of all time."},{"sourceSlug":"game-of-thrones","sourceName":"Game of Thrones","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-storm-of-swords","targetName":"A Storm of Swords","context":"The HBO series, which premiered in 2011, was adapted from the novel."},{"sourceSlug":"urban-fantasy","sourceName":"Urban Fantasy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-storm-of-swords","targetName":"A Storm of Swords","context":"A Storm of Swords' success helped pave the way for other urban fantasy series."},{"sourceSlug":"paranormal-romance","sourceName":"Paranormal Romance","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-storm-of-swords","targetName":"A Storm of Swords","context":"The novel's blend of fantasy and romance elements influenced the development of paranormal romance."},{"sourceSlug":"epic-fantasy","sourceName":"Epic Fantasy","verb":"DEFINES","targetSlug":"a-storm-of-swords","targetName":"A Storm of Swords","context":"A Storm of Swords is widely regarded as a landmark work in the epic fantasy genre."}],"places":[{"name":"New York City, USA","role":"Publication Site"},{"name":"London, UK","role":"Initial Publication"},{"name":"Westeros","role":"Setting"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Storm of Swords","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-storm-of-swords"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q431927","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Storm_of_Swords"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Storm of Swords contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Storm of Swords is the third novel in George R. R. Martin's A Song of Ice and Fire epic fantasy series, published on 8 August 2000 by Bantam Books — widely regarded by fans and critics as the best volume in the series, and the source of the series' most famous narrative event: the Red Wedding (the massacre of Robb Stark, his mother Catelyn Tully-Stark, and the Northern army at the wedding feast of Edmure Tully). With over 10 million copies sold, it is the best-selling volume of the series. A Storm of Swords covers the aftermath of the War of the Five Kings — the deaths of Robb Stark, Renly Baratheon, and Stannis's defeat at Blackwater — while advancing the story of Daenerys Targaryen's conquest of Essos, Jon Snow's experiences beyond the Wall with the wildlings, Tyrion Lannister's survival after the Battle of the Blackwater, and Jaime Lannister's transformation following the loss of his sword hand.

The Red Wedding — in which Walder Frey and Roose Bolton massacre Robb Stark and his Northern army at the wedding feast under the sacred protection of guest right — is the single most shocking narrative event in the A Song of Ice and Fire series and one of the most famous plot twists in the history of popular fiction. Its combination of narrative misdirection (the apparent security of guest right, the apparent political resolution of the Frey-Tully wedding), the scale of the betrayal, and the deaths of what had been presented as the main protagonist's family — coming after the series had already killed its apparent first protagonist (Ned Stark in book 1) — established Martin's narrative as uniquely willing to subvert the reader's expectations of heroic fantasy. The Red Wedding's HBO adaptation in Season 3 (Episode 9, 'The Rains of Castamere', directed by David Nutter) produced one of the most widely shared collective audience shock reactions in television history.

A Storm of Swords also contains the most concentrated sequence of major character transformations in the series: Jaime Lannister's redemption arc (the Oathbreaker who becomes the Oathkeeper), Arya Stark's journey toward the Faceless Men, Sansa's escape from King's Landing, Joffrey's assassination at his own wedding (the Purple Wedding), and Jon Snow's election as Lord Commander of the Night's Watch. The novel demonstrates Martin's narrative ambition at its fullest: a plot of extraordinary complexity in which no character's arc follows the expected heroic trajectory.","subjects":["Fantasy Fiction","Modern Era","George R. R. Martin","Epic Fantasy","Red Wedding","21st Century","American Literature","Television Adaptation"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Storm of Swords has had a profound impact on the fantasy genre and popular culture, influencing countless authors, television adaptations, and video games.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
